nodecode-mcp-creator is a rewrite of tonbistudio's tonbistudio/hermes-mcp-plugin-creator:

  https://github.com/tonbistudio/hermes-mcp-plugin-creator
  commit 0bcd7023ce9764a01551753a3b6800c4fe695047
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 tonbistudio (LICENSE)

The nine mcp_creator_* tools are rewritten in Common Lisp against Nodecode's
own seams, with the original's names and arguments: servers, introspect,
mine, convert, scaffold, validate, install, drift, review. What each one
keeps:

- introspect is the same one-shot, read-only MCP client: initialize, then
  tools/list, prompts/list and resources/list over stdio (a child's two
  pipes, newline-delimited JSON-RPC) or streamable HTTP (one POST per
  message, JSON or SSE back), with a fingerprint over the tool names and
  their input schemas, saved as the drift baseline.
- mine folds what the model actually did with a server: per-tool call and
  error counts, error samples, and which tool a call followed. The
  original read the profile's session database and its own hook log; here a
  :TOOL hook records the MCP calls an eval form makes, redacted, in this
  folder's own log.
- convert is the same portable-entry rewrite: a literal secret becomes
  ${ITS_NAME}, and an unpinned npx/uvx launcher, a profile-only key or an
  absolute path in an argument is a warning a publish build must fix.
- scaffold enforces the same name, pin and secret rules, is gated on its
  own marker file, scans for private detail, and refuses a publish build
  that carries any.
- validate, install, drift and review keep their meanings: a structural
  check of the package, a copy into the cells folder that refuses a folder
  it did not make, the unchanged/additive/recompile verdict, and a review
  over drift plus usage since the build.

Left out, because Nodecode's own machinery already is it: the plugin.json
and mcp.json package shape (the package written here is a Nodecode knowledge
cell, nodecode-<name>.asd plus a prose define-skill, with mcp.json beside
it for any MCP client), the `hermes plugins validate` and `hermes plugins
enable` subprocesses (validate is the cell's own structural check, install
writes into this home's cells folder and says the next boot loads it), and
the pre_tool_call approval gate (Nodecode asks its own questions before a
tool runs). The bundled twelve-step procedure, its references and the
skill's own frontmatter survive as the cell's manual, (help :mcp-creator).
